1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,293
Where is everybody?

2
00:00:01,593 --> 00:00:03,380
Our galaxy should be full of aliens.

3
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:07,598
It has hundreds of billions of stars, most with planets,

4
00:00:07,598 --> 00:00:09,610
and it's over 10 billion years old.

5
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:13,380
Even at 1% of light speed, a civilization

6
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:16,150
could cross it in about 10 million years.

7
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:18,618
So why has no one shown up?

8
00:00:18,618 --> 00:00:19,978
That's the Fermi paradox.

9
00:00:20,278 --> 00:00:21,800
Here are the 3 best answers.

10
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,339
1: we're too far apart.

11
00:00:24,339 --> 00:00:29,090
Our radio signals have only filled a bubble about 200 light-years wide.

12
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:31,500
2: we've barely looked.

13
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,116
Our searches so far are like checking one hot tub of water,

14
00:00:35,116 --> 00:00:36,710
out of all Earth's oceans.

15
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:38,820
3: the Great Filter.

16
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:41,874
Some step on the way to the stars is almost impossible,

17
00:00:42,174 --> 00:00:43,950
and it may still be ahead of us.

18
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:48,040
Until we know which one is true, the question stays open.

